  "headline": "Bangladesh Cabinet approves proposal to relocate Secretariat to Sher-e-Bangla Nagar",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The Bangladesh Cabinet has approved a proposal to relocate the Secretariat from Abdul Gani Road in the capital to Sher-e-Bangla Nagar, the site of the former trade fair grounds. The decision was made during a meeting chaired by Prime Minister Tarique Rahman at the Secretariat on Sunday afternoon, according to a report by Prothom Alo English. Cabinet Secretary Nasimul Ghani announced the decision during a subsequent press conference. This follows a previous decision in principle to relocate the Secretariat that was also made during a meeting chaired by the prime minister. Government offices are expected to begin operating at the new location in 2029."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Growing fears in Europe regarding the spillover of Russia’s war in Ukraine",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "European nations have expressed growing fears that Russia’s war in Ukraine could spread regionally and spill over into neighboring NATO members. Some countries argue the conflict has already expanded, citing instances of sabotage, cyberattacks, and drone incursions. European officials state that Moscow is targeting allies of Ukraine to facilitate intimidation, though the Kremlin denies these accusations. Polish Foreign Minister Radoslaw Sikorski has warned that Europe must be prepared to respond robustly to Russia’s hybrid attacks. Poland, which borders Ukraine, serves as a NATO logistics hub for both military and civilian support to its neighbor. The International Centre for Counterterrorism has identified Poland as the primary focus of Russia’s sabotage campaign in Europe. Additionally, research from Harvard indicates Poland is the country most targeted by cyberattacks, experiencing approximately four thousand politically motivated attacks per day. Following several reported airspace violations, Polish authorities have stated that further drone and missile strikes are likely. Sikorski noted that for the war to conclude, Russian President Vladimir Putin would have to admit that the full-scale invasion of Ukraine was a mistake and that his original goals are unachievable."
    }
  ]
}
